<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor packaging''' turns a manufactured chip into a component that can be connected, cooled, handled and used. A package carries signals and power between the tiny chip and a circuit board, supports the chip physically, and helps remove heat.</onlyinclude>

'''Reading depth:''' [[Semiconductor packaging/Simple|Simple]] · [[Semiconductor packaging/Technical|Technical]]

== How it works ==
A chip can be attached to a substrate and connected using very fine wires or microscopic solder connections. A protective material or lid surrounds the assembly. The finished package can be tested and attached to a circuit board.

== Why it matters ==
A chip can function electrically yet fail as a product if its connections crack, its temperature rises too far or its package absorbs moisture before soldering. Some advanced packages place several chips side by side on a dense interposer (often called 2.5D) or stack chips vertically (3D). These arrangements can shorten some connections, but cooling and reliability become harder.

== A useful distinction ==
'''Wafer fabrication''' builds electronic devices in semiconductor material. '''Packaging''' connects, protects and integrates the fabricated dies. They are related manufacturing stages, not synonyms.

